WEBVTT

00:00.540 --> 00:02.910
- [Instructor] Let's say
that we wanted to multiply

00:02.910 --> 00:07.410
five x squared and,
I'll do this in purple,

00:07.410 --> 00:11.260
three x to the fifth,
what would this equal?

00:11.260 --> 00:12.940
Pause this video and see if you can

00:12.940 --> 00:15.160
reason through that a little bit.

00:15.160 --> 00:16.980
All right, now let's work
through this together.

00:16.980 --> 00:18.660
And really, all we're going to do

00:18.660 --> 00:20.710
is use properties of multiplication

00:20.710 --> 00:22.550
and use properties of exponents

00:22.550 --> 00:25.040
to essentially rewrite this expression.

00:25.040 --> 00:26.640
So we can just view this,

00:26.640 --> 00:28.780
if we're just multiplying
a bunch of things,

00:28.780 --> 00:31.230
it doesn't matter what
order we multiply them in.

00:31.230 --> 00:33.540
So you can just view this
as five times x squared

00:33.540 --> 00:35.830
times three times x to the fifth,

00:35.830 --> 00:39.760
or we could multiply our
five and three first,

00:39.760 --> 00:44.760
so you could view this as
five times three, times three,

00:44.960 --> 00:48.720
times x squared, times x squared,

00:48.720 --> 00:52.890
times x to the fifth,
times x to the fifth.

00:52.890 --> 00:55.870
And now what is five times three?

00:55.870 --> 00:58.400
I think you know that, that is 15.

00:58.400 --> 01:02.669
Now what is x squared
times x to the fifth?

01:02.669 --> 01:04.472
Now some of you might recognize

01:04.472 --> 01:06.970
that exponent properties
would come into play here.

01:06.970 --> 01:09.290
If I'm multiplying two things like this,

01:09.290 --> 01:11.470
so we have the some base
and different exponents,

01:11.470 --> 01:13.960
that this is going to
be equal to x to the,

01:13.960 --> 01:16.100
and we add these two exponents,

01:16.100 --> 01:20.030
x to the two plus five power,
or x to the seventh power.

01:20.030 --> 01:22.240
If what I just did seems
counterintuitive to you

01:22.240 --> 01:24.500
I'll just remind you, what is x squared?

01:24.500 --> 01:27.030
x squared is x times x.

01:27.030 --> 01:28.840
And what is x to the fifth?

01:28.840 --> 01:33.840
That is x times x times x times x times x.

01:34.520 --> 01:38.360
And if you multiply them all
together what do you get?

01:38.360 --> 01:39.760
Well you got seven x's

01:39.760 --> 01:41.380
and you multiply them all together

01:41.380 --> 01:43.600
and that is x to the seventh.

01:43.600 --> 01:44.740
And so there you have it,

01:44.740 --> 01:46.550
five x squared times three x to the fifth

01:46.550 --> 01:49.840
is 15x to the seventh power.

01:49.840 --> 01:52.650
So the key is, is look
at these coefficients,

01:52.650 --> 01:55.680
look at these numbers, a five
and a three, multiply those,

01:55.680 --> 01:57.880
and then for any variable you have,

01:57.880 --> 02:00.460
you have x here, so
you have a common base,

02:00.460 --> 02:03.220
then you can add those exponents,

02:03.220 --> 02:06.770
and what we just did is known
as multiplying monomials,

02:06.770 --> 02:11.770
which sounds very fancy, but
this is a monomial, monomial,

02:12.440 --> 02:13.880
and in the future we'll do

02:13.880 --> 02:15.720
multiplying things like polynomials

02:15.720 --> 02:18.840
where we have multiple of
these things added together.

02:18.840 --> 02:21.610
But that's all it is,
multiplying monomials.

02:21.610 --> 02:22.860
Let's do one more example,

02:22.860 --> 02:24.810
and let's use a different
variable this time,

02:24.810 --> 02:27.370
just to get some variety in there.

02:27.370 --> 02:29.468
Let's say we wanna multiply the monomial

02:29.468 --> 02:32.540
three t to the seventh power,

02:32.540 --> 02:37.310
times another monomial negative four t.

02:37.310 --> 02:39.984
Pause this video and see if
you can work through that.

02:39.984 --> 02:43.480
All right, so I'm gonna do
this one a little bit faster.

02:43.480 --> 02:46.750
I am going to look at the
three and the negative four

02:46.750 --> 02:48.590
and I'm gonna multiply those first,

02:48.590 --> 02:50.750
and I'm going to get a negative 12.

02:50.750 --> 02:53.930
And then if I were to want to multiply

02:53.930 --> 02:56.960
the t to the seventh times t,

02:56.960 --> 03:00.290
once again they're both
the variable t as our base,

03:00.290 --> 03:02.250
so that's going to be t to the seventh

03:02.250 --> 03:05.290
times t to the first
power, that's what t is,

03:05.290 --> 03:08.590
that's going to be t to
the seven plus one power,

03:08.590 --> 03:10.030
or t to the eighth.

03:10.030 --> 03:12.170
But there you go, we are done again,

03:12.170 --> 03:14.993
we just multiplied
another set of monomials.
